@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# Product review research
+
+## Request paths and current behavior
+
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/product/ProductReviewApi.java` owns the v1 create, public list, update, and delete endpoints.
+- POST currently accepts both private and auth paths, checks duplicate reviews before overwriting the path product id, reports duplicates as 500, reports ratings above 5 as 503, and catches all exceptions as 503. It does not enforce that the submitted customer is the authenticated customer on the auth path.
+- `@Min(1)`/`@Max(5)` on `ProductReviewEntity.rating` permit fractional values and Bean Validation normally produces 400 only for request binding failures. The controller also only checks the upper bound, so ratings below 1 and non-whole values are not consistently rejected.
+- PUT currently loads a review by id, compares the submitted customer id rather than merchant ownership, does not verify the URL product matches the review product, and passes a newly populated review to the facade. Its path variable is declared `{reviewid}` while the Java parameter is `reviewId`, which is fragile without parameter-name discovery.
+- DELETE verifies the URL product but the facade calls the generic review delete without updating product aggregates. Missing or cross-product reviews should remain 404.
+
+## Data exposure
+
+- `ReadableProductReview` extends `ProductReviewEntity` and adds `ReadableCustomer customer`.
+- `ReadableProductReviewPopulator` uses `ReadableCustomerPopulator`, which exposes email, billing address, delivery address, last name, groups and attributes. This is the source of the public privacy leak.
+- The public review contract needs a deliberately small reviewer representation (first name only), while still retaining review id, rating, description, date, and the existing product/language fields only if compatible with the endpoint contract. No customer object containing the full customer DTO should be populated.
+
+## Persistence and rating calculation
+
+- `ProductReviewRepository` has a unique `(CUSTOMERS_ID, PRODUCT_ID)` constraint and `findByProductAndCustomer`, so the database also protects the one-review rule, but the API currently converts the conflict to 500.
+- `ProductReviewServiceImpl.saveOrUpdate` calculates a new average from the cached product average/count and always increments the count, even for updates. It saves the review and then the product. It has no delete override/recalculation.
+- Product aggregates are `Product.productReviewAvg` and `Product.productReviewCount`; v1/v2 product mappers expose them. Several mappers round to half-star increments with `Math.round(avg * 2) / 2.0`, but the requirement is one decimal place with halves rounded up, so the persisted aggregate should be calculated as a `BigDecimal` scale 1 with `RoundingMode.HALF_UP`. Empty products must persist/display zero values.
+- `ProductReview` has a customer and product association and review descriptions cascade from the review. The product review service is the correct place to recalculate aggregates for create, update, and delete so all callers remain consistent.
+
+## Authentication and affected areas
+
+- JWT customer authentication creates `JWTUser`/`CustomerDetails`, both carrying the customer id; the authenticated principal is available through Spring Security. The auth review POST must compare that id to `review.customerId` and return 403 before persistence.
+- `ProductCommonFacadeImpl` populates a core `ProductReview` and delegates create/update/delete to `ProductReviewService`; update currently creates a new core object rather than loading/merging the existing one, so it must be changed or the API/service must perform a true update.
+- `ReadableProductMapper`, `ReadableMinimalProductMapper`, and the v2 product-definition mapper consume the cached aggregates. The v2 definition mapper has the review mapping commented out, so the `/api/v2/product/{sku}` response path must be checked and enabled/fixed as needed.
+- Existing integration coverage only has an ignored private create test. New focused tests should cover DTO privacy, validation/status codes, duplicate/ownership refusal, cross-product not-found behavior, and aggregate transitions.
